    "source": {
      "authors": [
        "Rui Faria",
        "Miguel Azenha",
        "Joaquim A. Figueiras"
      ],
      "year": 2006,
      "journal": "Cement & Concrete Composites",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.cemconcomp.2006.02.012"
    },
    "dimension": 2,
    "physics_class": "thermo-chemo-mechanical",
    "validation_method": "digitized paper curves: Fig. 9a (DPL on), Fig. 17 (2D/3D P3), Fig. 18 (\u03b5x bottom)",
    "validation_target": "\u03c3x(t) at 5 cm from top / mid-plane / 5 cm from bottom over 9.2 d (Fig. 9a); secondary overlay of Fig. 17 and Fig. 18",
    "notes": "RISKY: (1) Affinity B1/B2/eta/Hu/alpha_inf are NOT in the paper (Fig. 5 multi-component only) \u2014 peak T will not match Fig. 18 until those are fitted. (2) E=29 GPa * sqrt(alpha) is LEGACY-ONLY; this file uses constant E=29 GPa and OOFEM will ignore alpha_0/a_E. (3) This mesh is the Sec. 4.2 'infinite slab' (\u03b5x=\u03b5z=0), which is Fig. 9. Fig. 17 P3 is ~86% of that stress; Fig. 18 is a 3D sensor with finite pile restraint \u2014 expect the same shape, not the same amplitude. (4) Air history is missing; top u_ref=17 \u00b0C constant. (5) power_law_creep maps Table 2 DPL (phi1=2.26, m=0.35, n=0.3) onto doublepowerlaw; confirm your builder keys (E28/fi1/m/n). Do NOT add thermal_aging or b3_creep."
  },
